.TH HOSTNAME 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hostname \- show or set the system host name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostname
.RB [ \-f | \-s ]
.br
.B hostname
.B \-F
.I file
.br
.B hostname
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I name
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no operand,
.B hostname
prints the kernel host name.
By default only the part before the first dot is printed; use
.B \-f
to print the whole name.
No DNS lookup is performed in either case.
.PP
If the kernel host name is empty, or is
.B (none)
or
.BR localhost ,
.B hostname
reads the first line of
.IR /etc/hostname .
If that line is non-empty,
.B hostname
also sets it as the kernel host name, ignoring any error, and prints it.
This lets the name be shown correctly early in boot, before the
host name has been set by the boot scripts.
.PP
With a
.I name
operand,
.B hostname
sets the kernel host name to
.I name
with
.BR sethostname (2).
.SH OPTIONS
Options are processed from left to right.
.TP
.B \-f
Print the full host name, including any domain part.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the short host name, up to the first dot (the default).
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Set the host name from the first line of
.IR file ,
with trailing newline, carriage-return, space and tab characters
removed.
It is an error if the file is empty or the line is blank.
.B hostname
exits as soon as this option has been handled; any later arguments
are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options; the next argument is taken as
.IR name .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option was given,
.B \-F
had no argument,
.I file
could not be read or was blank, or getting or setting the host name
failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hostname
Read when the kernel host name is unset or
.BR localhost ,
and conventionally used with
.BR \-F .
.SH EXAMPLES
Set the host name from the configuration file:
.PP
.EX
# hostname \-F /etc/hostname
.EE
.PP
Print the fully qualified name:
.PP
.EX
$ hostname \-f
build.example.org
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uname (1),
.BR sys_hostname (2),
.BR uname (2)
.SH BUGS
Merely printing the host name can change it, as described above.
.PP
The Substrate kernel currently does not check privilege in
.BR sethostname (2),
so any user can change the host name.
Names longer than the kernel limit are silently truncated.
